WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.491
Three robot arms share one table.

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.913
Practicing here is slow to reset,

3
00:00:04.913 --> 00:00:08.226
and with several arms in one workspace, it is unsafe.

4
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.041
CoEnv rebuilds the workcell as a physics twin,

5
00:00:12.041 --> 00:00:14.155
from calibrated depth cameras.

6
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.931
Inside the twin, a coding agent writes one program for all three arms,

7
00:00:18.931 --> 00:00:19.715
and runs it.

8
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.025
Before risky steps,

9
00:00:21.025 --> 00:00:24.166
checkpoints save the state and several rendered views,

10
00:00:24.166 --> 00:00:27.365
and a verifier checks every step against the simulator.

11
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.303
Then the agent rewrites and reruns the program,

12
00:00:30.303 --> 00:00:32.144
for up to three rounds in total.

13
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.238
Only verified programs reach the real robots,

14
00:00:35.238 --> 00:00:36.752
after a collision check.

15
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.692
On five real multi-arm tasks,

16
00:00:43.692 --> 00:00:47.510
the coding agent reaches seventy percent average success,

17
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.547
against fifty percent when a vision-language model picks every step.

18
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.440
Against two prior planning frameworks,

19
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.095
the coding agent reaches seventy-seven percent on three tasks,

20
00:00:58.095 --> 00:00:59.923
compared with twenty and forty.

21
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.683
CoEnv can also collect real multi-arm data,

22
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:08.194
by re-running verified programs from new starting positions.

23
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.891
Trained only on this data,

24
00:01:10.891 --> 00:01:16.409
a π0.5 policy outperforms one trained on twenty teleoperated demos.
